NAME
    filesystem - paths, permissions, trash, and encryption

DESCRIPTION
    Every user-level filesystem operation goes through securefs, which enforces
    per-user access control. Paths are normalized first (., .., backslashes, and
    NUL bytes are all handled; a tainted path fails closed), so ACL checks can't
    be tricked by clever path strings.

LAYOUT & PERMISSIONS
    /public        everyone reads; USER+ writes
    /home/<user>   the owner (or an admin) only
    /root          root only
    /tmp           everyone read/write
    /tos /etc /var /usr   system: users read, ADMIN+ writes (with a protected
                          core that even admins can't overwrite via securefs)

TRASH & RECOVERY
    rm moves a file to your per-user trash (use 'rm --hard' to skip it).
      trash            list / empty your trash
      restore <file>   undelete from trash

ENCRYPTION AT REST
    vault encrypt/decrypt <path>   passphrase-based file/tape/in-place encryption
    keychain                       per-user passphrase stash (login-unlocked)

INTEGRITY
    Critical state files are written atomically (temp then replace), so a power
    cut mid-save cannot truncate them; interrupted writes are repaired at boot.

SEE ALSO
    man vault, ls, cp, rm, MANUAL.md (Chapter 5)
